---
import Island from "@/components/Island.astro"
import "@/styles/global.css"
import "@/styles/island.css"
import "@/styles/page.css"

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "")
const media = (f: string) => `${base}/media/${f}`
const repo = "https://github.com/Tejas242/ember"
const title = "Ember: a dynamic island for Wayland"
const description =
  "A small pill that hangs from your bar. It knows what Claude Code is doing, asks before it runs anything, plays your music and falls asleep at night. One Go binary, drawn in software."
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#070908" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:image" content={new URL(media("social.png"), Astro.site).href} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <link rel="icon" href={`${base}/favicon.svg`} type="image/svg+xml" />
    <script is:inline>document.documentElement.classList.add("js")</script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="bar" aria-label="Site">
      <a class="brand" href="#top" aria-label="Ember, top of the page">
        <i></i><i></i><i></i>
        <span>ember</span>
      </a>
      <nav>
        <a href="#install">Install</a>
        <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/DESIGN.md`}>Design</a>
        <a href={repo} class="gh">GitHub</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <Island />

    <div class="thread" aria-hidden="true"><i data-thread></i></div>

    <main id="top">
      <section class="hero wrap" data-island="clock">
        <div class="hero-text" data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow">A dynamic island for Wayland</p>
          <h1>The top of your screen, <span class="em">paying attention.</span></h1>
          <p class="lede">
            Ember is a small pill that hangs from your bar. It knows what Claude Code is doing, asks you before it
            runs anything, plays your music and falls asleep when the desk goes quiet.
          </p>
          <div class="ctas">
            <a class="cta primary" href={repo}>
              <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 .2a8 8 0 0 0-2.5 15.6c.4 0 .5-.2.5-.4v-1.5c-2.2.5-2.7-1-2.7-1-.4-.9-.9-1.2-.9-1.2-.7-.5.1-.5.1-.5.8.1 1.2.8 1.2.8.7 1.3 1.9.9 2.3.7.1-.5.3-.9.5-1.1-1.8-.2-3.6-.9-3.6-4 0-.9.3-1.6.8-2.1-.1-.2-.4-1 .1-2.1 0 0 .7-.2 2.2.8a7.5 7.5 0 0 1 4 0c1.5-1 2.2-.8 2.2-.8.4 1.1.2 1.9.1 2.1.5.6.8 1.3.8 2.1 0 3.1-1.9 3.8-3.6 4 .3.3.6.8.6 1.5v2.2c0 .2.1.5.6.4A8 8 0 0 0 8 .2"/></svg>
              View on GitHub
            </a>
            <a class="cta" href="#install">Install it <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
          </div>
          <p class="facts mono">one go binary <i></i> drawn in software <i></i> about 9 MB <i></i> nothing polls</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="mascot" data-reveal>
          <div class="halo" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <ember-flame mood="idle" data-energy="0.55"></ember-flame>
          <figcaption>It blinks about fifteen times a minute. Nobody asked it to.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p class="hint" data-reveal><span aria-hidden="true">↑</span> <span class="hover">Hover</span><span class="tap">Tap</span> the island up there. It drops a card.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter wrap" data-island="request" id="requests">
        <div class="copy" data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow"><b>01</b> Does anything need me?</p>
          <h2>Claude asks. You answer from the top of the screen.</h2>
          <p>
            When Claude wants to run a command or edit a file, the request drops out of the island: Claude's own words
            as the title, the whole command, and a quiet line saying exactly what <em>Always</em> would allow, before you
            press it.
          </p>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>The command wraps instead of being cut off, so the end, where an <code>&amp;&amp; rm -rf</code> would sit, is always on screen.</li>
            <li>Questions from Claude come as a numbered list. Requests that pile up stack under the card.</li>
            <li>Ember nods when you allow and shakes its head when you deny. It has opinions about neither.</li>
            <li>If you're busy it waits 85 seconds, then Claude asks in the terminal, the way it always did.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="aside"><span aria-hidden="true">↑</span> It's asking right now. Try Allow.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="frame" data-reveal>
          <img src={media("request.png")} alt="A request drawn by Ember: KINVAULT WANTS TO RUN, the title Run the store tests with the race detector, the command go test -race ./store/... -run TestRepair -count=1 wrapped onto two lines, and Answer in terminal, Deny, Always and Allow" loading="lazy" width="840" height="344" />
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter wrap flip" data-island="card" id="timeline">
        <div class="copy" data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow"><b>02</b> Is it going well?</p>
          <h2>The work as it happens, hanging from a thread.</h2>
          <p>
            One session is the hero: the one waiting on you, else the one working where you can't see it. Its task in
            words, one line of facts, and its latest steps hanging from Ember on a thin thread. Each new step arrives
            with a bead of light running down from the flame.
          </p>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>A failed step says so in red. A failure that was retried and fixed is called a retry, because that's what it was.</li>
            <li>Subagents branch off the thread, each opening into its own steps.</li>
            <li>Every other session is one line: <span class="g">Done ·</span> <span class="r">Stopped ·</span> <span class="b">Needs you ·</span>. Click one to make it the hero.</li>
            <li>A session silent for three minutes turns amber. It might be a long build. It might be a hang. Either way, worth a look.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <figure class="frame" data-reveal>
          <img src={media("card.png")} alt="The card, hanging from the bar: the task as a headline, one line of facts, five steps on a thread with a failed test in red and a subagent branching off, the song playing on a haze of its cover's colours, and the practice line with today's ring" loading="lazy" width="840" height="692" />
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter wrap narrow" data-island="done" id="done">
        <div class="copy center" data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow"><b>03</b> What happened?</p>
          <h2>Done, in one sentence, while you were reading something else.</h2>
          <p>
            When a session finishes where you can't see it, the pill says so with the most informative sentence of
            Claude's reply. Outcomes and numbers beat preamble, so you get <em>“all 42 tests pass under -race”</em> and
            not <em>“Great question!”</em>. A check draws itself. If it stopped on an error you get the error in words,
            like <em>rate limited</em>, and the outcome waits beside the clock until you've seen it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="minis" data-reveal-group aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="mini"><i class="dot g"></i><b class="g">Done</b> kinvault <em>All 42 tests pass under -race</em><svg viewBox="0 0 12 10"><path d="M1 5.2 4.3 8.4 11 1.4" /></svg></span>
          <span class="then">then, until you look</span>
          <span class="mini"><i class="dot"></i>5:26 PM <em class="g">kinvault done</em></span>
          <span class="then">or, if it went wrong</span>
          <span class="mini"><i class="dot r"></i><b class="r">Stopped</b> kinvault <em>Rate limited</em></span>
        </div>
        <figure class="frame story" data-reveal>
          <img src={media("claude.webp")} alt="Ember on a desktop: the pill names the session at work, the card drops with its live timeline, a request to run the store tests is allowed from the island, and the pill says Done with Claude's summary" loading="lazy" width="1520" height="660" />
          <figcaption>The whole story, fourteen seconds long, drawn frame by frame by Ember's own renderer.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter wrap duo" id="desk">
        <div class="copy center" data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow"><b>04</b> The rest of the desk</p>
          <h2>Music, volume, headphones, screenshots.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="media-pair">
          <figure class="frame tall" data-island="music" data-reveal>
            <img src={media("music.webp")} alt="The music demo: the cover in a ring in the pill, then the card's now playing section on a haze of the cover's colours, pause and play, and the volume rising to 100" loading="lazy" width="880" height="420" />
            <figcaption>
              <b>The equaliser listens.</b> While a song plays, Ember reads the output's levels at 8 kHz and splits them
              into low, mid and high, 25 times a second. It keeps three numbers and forgets the song.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="frame tall" data-island="volume" data-reveal>
            <img src={media("moments.webp")} alt="The moments demo: charging with the time until full, headphones connecting with their battery, a screenshot with a shutter flash, brightness falling, then a session waiting with a blue halo" loading="lazy" width="800" height="96" />
            <figcaption>
              <b>Moments</b> step in and step aside: the volume's waves come and go with the level, the sun's rays grow
              with the brightness, and at 100 the meter stretches, so you can feel there's no 101.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter wrap mind" data-island="clock" id="mind">
        <div class="copy center" data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow"><b>05</b> It has a small mind</p>
          <h2>None of it is scripted animation.</h2>
          <p>A handful of models run on real time. The flame is just where they show.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid4" data-reveal-group>
          <article class="tile">
            <svg class="viz energy" viewBox="0 0 120 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0 34 C14 34 20 30 30 26 S46 8 60 8 S84 20 96 26 S112 34 120 33" /><circle cx="60" cy="8" r="2.6" /></svg>
            <h3>Affect</h3>
            <p>Energy and mood relax toward a baseline set by the hour: highest mid-afternoon, lowest at 3 am. A finished task cheers it up. An error sulks for a while.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="tile">
            <div class="viz eyes" aria-hidden="true"><ember-flame mood="idle" data-energy="0.3"></ember-flame></div>
            <h3>Life</h3>
            <p>Blinks are a Poisson process, sometimes doubled. It glances at things as they appear, gets drowsy on a quiet desk, and falls asleep with z's rising.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="tile">
            <div class="viz away" aria-hidden="true"><span>While you were away</span><em>kinvault done · ember asked you</em></div>
            <h3>Presence</h3>
            <p>It knows when you've stepped away. News that arrives meanwhile isn't shown to an empty room. You get it in one line when you're back.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="tile">
            <div class="viz bars" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--h:.85"></i><i style="--h:.4"></i><i style="--h:.25"></i><i style="--h:.55"></i></div>
            <h3>Attention</h3>
            <p>What the pill shows is scored, not hard-coded, with hysteresis: a choice holds until a rival clearly beats it, so it never flickers between two close calls.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter wrap tool" data-island="work" id="tool">
        <div class="copy" data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow"><b>06</b> Built like a small tool</p>
          <h2>No toolkit, no GPU, and nothing that polls.</h2>
          <p>
            Ember talks the Wayland protocol directly and draws every pixel itself, in Go, into shared memory. While
            nothing moves, only the flame's few pixels and the live parts repaint. Everything it knows arrives as an
            event: Claude Code's hooks over a socket, D-Bus for music and the battery, inotify for the backlight and
            screenshots, niri's own event stream for windows.
          </p>
        </div>
        <dl class="numbers" data-reveal-group>
          <div><dt>memory, private</dt><dd>≈9 <small>MB</small></dd></div>
          <div><dt>of one core while Claude works</dt><dd>≈1<small>%</small></dd></div>
          <div><dt>binaries</dt><dd>1</dd></div>
          <div><dt>polling loops</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <figure class="frame wide" data-reveal>
          <img src={media("dash.webp")} alt="The edge plugin's dashboard opening: a ring for the day's goal, the problem in progress with its hint ladder, the week's bars, a Codeforces rating line with a soft fill, a year of practice as a heatmap and four numbers counting up" loading="lazy" width="1520" height="760" />
          <figcaption>Plugins sleep until their tool is installed. The first is edge, a practice coach, with a dashboard that opens in a short choreography.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter wrap narrow" data-island="clock" id="install">
        <div class="copy center" data-reveal>
          <p class="eyebrow"><b>07</b> Install</p>
          <h2>Three lines, one of which is a check-up.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="terminal" data-reveal>
          <div class="tbar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><button type="button" data-copy>Copy</button></div>
          <pre><code><span class="c"># a Wayland compositor with layer-shell, Go 1.27+, systemd</span>
git clone https://github.com/Tejas242/ember &amp;&amp; cd ember
make install    <span class="c"># the binary, a user service, Claude Code's hooks, a config</span>
ember doctor    <span class="c"># says what's missing, and how to get it</span></code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="small center" data-reveal>
          Built for <b>niri</b> with the <b>Noctalia</b> shell; anything with <code>zwlr_layer_shell_v1</code> should do.
          Music needs an MPRIS player, the equaliser PipeWire. The <a href={`${repo}#config`}>config</a> takes effect
          within ten seconds, without a restart.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="foot wrap">
      <ember-flame mood="idle" data-energy="0.3"></ember-flame>
      <p>
        Ember is <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>MIT licensed</a>. Made by
        <a href="https://screenager.dev">Tejas Mahajan</a>, who now finds out his tests passed from the top of the
        screen.
      </p>
      <nav><a href={repo}>GitHub</a><a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/DESIGN.md`}>Design</a><a href={`${repo}/issues`}>Issues</a></nav>
    </footer>

    <script>
      import "@/scripts/page"
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
